To understand the origin of the "量子真空" (Quantum Vacuum) or what existed before it, we must step to the very edge of modern theoretical physics.
In classical physics, a vacuum is just an empty container. But in modern physics, the Quantum Vacuum is already a highly complex "something"—it is the lowest energy state (the ground state) of quantum fields.
Because it is already a structured physical entity, physicists have proposed deeper layers of reality that predate or underly it. Here is what lies before and beneath the quantum vacuum, divided into its historical origin and its cosmological predecessor.

1. The Predecessor: What existed before the Quantum Vacuum?

Before the structured quantum vacuum emerged, theoretical models suggest foundational states where traditional spacetime may not have applied:
• Pure Quantum Information: Emerging spacetime theories propose that an entangled network of abstract qubits existed prior to space, time, and fields, with the quantum vacuum emerging from this foundational information.
• Quantum Foam & Planck Scales: At sub-Planck scales, smooth fields break down, representing a chaotic geometry where stable vacuum states have not yet formed. [1] (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/26541358_The_Source_of_the_Quantum_Vacuum)
• Prior Cosmic Cycles: In cyclic or bouncing cosmologies, a prior universal phase collapses or dilutes before resetting into a new vacuum state. [1] (https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/17721/physical-laws-prior-the-big-bang-quantum-fluctuations), [2] (https://arxiv.org/pdf/physics/0408111)

